In C:/projects/hometongue/sw.js, replace the fetch handler body at lines 44-62 (keep the early returns at lines 38-42 exactly as they are) with a cache-race: look the cache up first, still let the network win whenever it answers, but hand over the cached copy if the network has not answered within a short deadline.

  // Network first so a deploy lands immediately — but only for as long as the network is
  // actually moving. Signal with no throughput (subway, elevator, packed venue) does not
  // reject; it hangs until the browser gives up, and a cached shell beats a blank screen.
  // Only healthy responses are cached — a 500 stored here once would replay forever. And the
  // index fallback is for NAVIGATIONS only: serving index.html bytes to a failed script or CSS
  // request produces far stranger breakage than a plain failure.
  const NET_DEADLINE_MS = 3000;

  e.respondWith((async () => {
    const cached = await caches.match(e.request);

    const network = fetch(e.request).then((res) => {
      if (res.ok) {
        const copy = res.clone();
        caches.open(VERSION).then((c) => c.put(e.request, copy));
      }
      return res;
    });
    e.waitUntil(network.catch(() => {}));   // let the revalidate finish; swallow the rejection

    if (cached) {
      const timeout = new Promise((r) => { setTimeout(() => r(null), NET_DEADLINE_MS); });
      const winner = await Promise.race([network.catch(() => null), timeout]);
      return winner || cached;
    }

    try {
      return await network;
    } catch {
      if (e.request.mode === 'navigate') {
        const shell = await caches.match('/');
        if (shell) return shell;
      }
      return Response.error();
    }
  })());

Notes for whoever applies it:
- e.waitUntil(network.catch(...)) is required. Without it the SW can be killed mid-revalidate after the cached copy is returned, and the entry never refreshes.
- The two .catch() calls are not redundant: the one inside Promise.race converts a rejection to null so a fast failure (true offline) falls through to the cached copy instead of rejecting the race; the one in waitUntil prevents an unhandled rejection.
- The trade-off is that a genuinely slow but working connection (>3s) now serves a stale shell for one load. That is already covered: sw.js re-puts every healthy response so the entry self-refreshes, and js/app.js:1316-1330 polls the /sw.js ETag on visibilitychange and reloads into a new build when the UI is idle.
- Do NOT bump VERSION for this. Editing sw.js is itself a byte diff, which is what triggers the browser to install the new worker; a bump would only evict a cache that is already correct.
- Optional, and the thing that actually matters more for the lie-fi case this finding describes: the render-blocking cross-origin resources at index.html:22-23 / game.html:22-23 (Google Fonts CSS, Leaflet CSS) and the blocking script at index.html:174 / game.html:168 (Leaflet JS) still go straight to the stalled network and block paint regardless of this SW change. Self-hosting Leaflet under /js/ or /css/ and adding it to SHELL, or at minimum putting the Leaflet script behind defer, is what would actually get a first paint on a hanging connection.

