Two independent defects; fix both.

=== FIX 1: domain-separate the token so a game token cannot unlock storage (api/feedback.js) ===

Replace mintToken/tokenAge/tokenValid (feedback.js lines 30-59) with a purpose-bound version. Add timingSafeEqual to the node:crypto import on line 5.

  import { randomUUID, createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from 'node:crypto';

  export function mintToken(purpose = 'clip') {
    const secret = tokenSecret();
    if (!secret) return '';
    const ts = Date.now().toString();
    const sig = createHmac('sha256', secret).update(`${purpose}.${ts}`).digest('hex').slice(0, 32);
    return `${purpose}.${ts}.${sig}`;
  }

  function parseToken(token, purpose) {
    const secret = tokenSecret();
    if (!secret || typeof token !== 'string') return null;
    const parts = token.split('.');
    if (parts.length !== 3) return null;            // rejects every legacy 2-part token
    const [p, ts, sig] = parts;
    if (p !== purpose) return null;                 // THE FIX: a 'game' token cannot satisfy a 'clip' check
    if (!/^\d+$/.test(ts) || !/^[0-9a-f]{32}$/.test(sig)) return null;
    const expect = createHmac('sha256', secret).update(`${p}.${ts}`).digest('hex').slice(0, 32);
    if (!timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(sig), Buffer.from(expect))) return null;
    const age = Date.now() - Number(ts);
    return age >= 0 ? age : null;
  }

  export function tokenAge(token, purpose = 'game') { return parseToken(token, purpose); }
  function tokenValid(token) { const age = parseToken(token, 'clip'); return age !== null && age < TOKEN_MAX_AGE_MS; }

Update all four call sites (these are the only ones in the repo):
  - api/analyze.js:447  -> fb_token: mintToken('clip')
  - api/scores.js:57    -> res.end(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, token: mintToken('game') }))
  - api/scores.js:117   -> const age = tokenAge(b.token, 'game');
  - api/feedback.js:142 -> unchanged (tokenValid now demands purpose 'clip')

Correct the now-true-again comment on feedback.js:139, and note in scores.js:55-57 that the start token is deliberately storage-inert.

Deploy note: this invalidates tokens in flight. A player mid-game gets 422 invalid_token on submit and one in-flight clip donation is dropped. game.js:184 re-mints on game start and app.js:628 re-mints on every analyze, so it self-heals on reload. Accept that rather than adding a legacy-token grace path.

Recommended hardening on top (cheap, kills token farming even from /api/analyze): bind the clip token to the caller's IP. Pass the same platform-derived ip used at feedback.js:100 / analyze.js into mintToken and HMAC over `${purpose}.${ts}.${ip}`, then recompute with the request's own ip in parseToken. An attacker then has to actually spend a Gemini-costed, daily-capped analyze call from the same IP for each upload.

=== FIX 2: stop trusting the client-declared mime; sniff the container (api/feedback.js) ===

Domain separation alone still lets anyone who ran one real analyze push 2.5MB of arbitrary non-audio. Inside the block at line 144, after `const bytes = Buffer.from(b.audio, 'base64')` and the length check, require the magic bytes to match the declared container and reject otherwise (do not fall through to the insert with clip_path set):

  function sniff(buf) {
    if (buf.length < 12) return null;
    if (buf[0] === 0x1a && buf[1] === 0x45 && buf[2] === 0xdf && buf[3] === 0xa3) return 'webm'; // EBML
    if (buf.toString('ascii', 0, 4) === 'OggS') return 'ogg';
    if (buf.toString('ascii', 4, 8) === 'ftyp') return 'mp4';
    if (buf.toString('ascii', 0, 4) === 'RIFF' && buf.toString('ascii', 8, 12) === 'WAVE') return 'wav';
    if (buf.toString('ascii', 0, 3) === 'ID3') return 'mp3';
    if (buf[0] === 0xff && (buf[1] & 0xe0) === 0xe0) return 'mp3';                                // frame sync
    return null;
  }

Then gate the upload on `sniff(bytes) === AUDIO_MIME[baseMime]`, and derive the stored extension and row.mime from the sniffed result rather than from b.mime. Treat webm/ogg as interchangeable if MediaRecorder output requires it (both are EBML/Ogg containers; verify against a real Chrome and a real iOS Safari recording before shipping, since iOS emits audio/mp4).

All changes in api/scores.js. Do NOT add sameOrigin() to the GET path — browsers omit Origin on same-origin GET fetches and sameOrigin() rejects an empty origin, so it would 403 every real user; it is also spoofed with one curl header. Caching plus a read throttle are the actual fix.

1) Add a second, independent bucket for reads near the existing limiter (after line 27). Keep it separate from the POST limiter so a shared carrier NAT full of real phones cannot lock itself out of the board:

const readBuckets = new Map();
function readLimited(ip) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const b = readBuckets.get(ip) || { count: 0, reset: now + 600_000 };
  if (now > b.reset) { b.count = 0; b.reset = now + 600_000; }
  b.count += 1;
  readBuckets.set(ip, b);
  if (readBuckets.size > 5000) readBuckets.clear();
  return b.count > 300;
}

2) Inside `if (req.method === 'GET')`, set headers per exit and gate only the Supabase read:

- ?start=1 branch (line 57): add res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-store') before res.end. This is load-bearing, not hygiene: mintToken() (api/feedback.js:33) embeds Date.now() in the token and tokenAge gates submission at 75s (line 118), so a token cached 30s and reissued to the next player lets a 45-second run submit as legitimate.
- After that early return, derive the IP with the same expression as line 94 (x-real-ip first, never trust leftmost x-forwarded-for), then:
  if (readLimited(ip)) { res.setHeader('Cache-Control','no-store'); res.statusCode = 429; return res.end(JSON.stringify({ ok:false, error:'rate_limited' })); }
- Success path only, immediately before res.end at line 76: res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'public, s-maxage=30, stale-while-revalidate=300'). Set it here and nowhere higher — putting it at line 48 would cache the mint token and would pin a not_ready/500 at the edge for 30s.
- Give the not_configured exit (59), the not_ready exit (64) and the catch block (77-81) an explicit 'no-store' so a transient Supabase failure is not replayed to everyone for the next 30 seconds.

3) Both halves are required. Vercel keys its edge cache on the full URL including query string, so ?game_type=arabic&x=<random> busts the cache on every request. The s-maxage collapses all honest traffic (10 fixed URLs) to roughly 2 Supabase queries per minute per deck; readLimited is what caps the DB cost of a cache-busting loop. Neither alone suffices.

4) Out of scope for the file: the Vercel invocation cost cannot be stopped from inside the handler, since the invocation has already occurred by the time the code runs. If that line item matters on launch day, add a Vercel Firewall rate-limit rule on /api/* in the dashboard — a console change, optional. The code fix above removes the Supabase side entirely.

