In C:/projects/hometongue/js/app.js, move the handler assignments and the start() call inside the existing try, and null onstop in the catch so a partial stop can never reach onRecordingReady.

Replace lines 423-433:

  try {
    recorder = new MediaRecorder(mediaStream, mime ? { mimeType: mime } : undefined);
  } catch {
    stopTimer(); stopMeter(); teardownRecording();
    state = 'idle'; show('idleCard');
    toast('Recording failed to start on this browser. Try again, or type instead.');
    return;
  }
  recorder.ondataavailable = (e) => { if (e.data && e.data.size) chunks.push(e.data); };
  recorder.onstop = onRecordingReady;
  recorder.start(500);

with:

  try {
    recorder = new MediaRecorder(mediaStream, mime ? { mimeType: mime } : undefined);
    recorder.ondataavailable = (e) => { if (e.data && e.data.size) chunks.push(e.data); };
    recorder.onstop = onRecordingReady;
    // start() throws too, not just the constructor: Chromium raises UnknownError
    // ("There was an error starting the MediaRecorder") when the encoder fails to
    // initialise. Outside this try it became an unhandled rejection and left a
    // convincing fake recording running - meter and countdown are fed by the
    // AudioContext, not the recorder - which ended 20-60s later in
    // "I didn't catch any audio", blaming the user for a browser failure.
    recorder.start(500);
  } catch {
    if (recorder) recorder.onstop = null;
    stopTimer(); stopMeter(); teardownRecording();
    state = 'idle'; show('idleCard');
    toast('Recording failed to start on this browser. Try again, or type instead.');
    return;
  }

Two notes on correctness of the fix. teardownRecording() stops the tracks and sets recorder = null, but it must run after onstop is cleared, which the ordering above guarantees. And keep the assignments before start(), not after, so that a timeslice-driven ondataavailable cannot fire before the handler exists.

Optional hardening, same function, not required for this finding: also add recorder.onerror = () => { ... same cleanup + toast ... } to catch asynchronous recorder failures, and register a global window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', ...) so no future async onclick can fail invisibly - the repo currently has no such handler anywhere.

