In js/app.js `startListening`, make the 'starting' state escapable and generation-guarded rather than racing the permission prompt on a timer. A plain timeout is the tempting fix but it is a regression risk: people routinely take 10-20s to decide on a permission sheet, and a 15s timeout that fires while the sheet is still up would tear down the stream they are about to grant.

1. Add a module-level counter next to `let takeHandled = false;` (js/app.js:393):

   let gumGen = 0;

2. Replace the acquisition block at js/app.js:407-414. Note it currently assigns straight into the module-level `mediaStream`, which is itself a latent bug: a stale promise resolving during a later live take overwrites `mediaStream`, and the js/app.js:414 branch then nulls it, so `teardownRecording` can never stop the live take's tracks. Use a local:

   const myGen = ++gumGen;
   let stream;
   try {
     stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true });
   } catch {
     state = 'idle';
     toast('Mic blocked. Allow the microphone, or type instead.');
     enterTypeMode();
     return;
   }
   // Superseded (user escaped to type mode, or tapped again) — never touch mediaStream here.
   if (state !== 'starting' || myGen !== gumGen) {
     stream.getTracks().forEach((t) => t.stop());
     return;
   }
   mediaStream = stream;

   The `myGen !== gumGen` half is the part that is easy to miss: without it, a hung call that resolves after the user has retried would see state 'starting' again (set by the retry) and proceed as if it were the current take.

3. Give the wedged state an exit at the click site, js/app.js:1232:

   $('#micBtn').onclick = () => {
     if (state === 'starting') {          // permission prompt never came back
       state = 'idle';
       toast('Still waiting on the mic. Tap again to retry, or type instead.');
       return;
     }
     startListening();
   };

   With step 2 in place this is safe: bumping into a fresh `startListening` increments `gumGen`, so the abandoned promise is inert whenever it resolves.

4. Optional but worth it, since the same gap is what makes the wedge invisible: give the pending window any feedback at all. After `state = 'starting'` set a short timer (~400ms, so it never flickers on the already-granted fast path) that adds a class to #micBtn, and clear it on every exit from the block above. A dimmed or pulsing button during the permission sheet also helps the ordinary first-time visitor, who currently gets zero acknowledgement that their tap registered.

Add a polyfill to the top of C:/projects/hometongue/js/theme.js — insert immediately after the header comment and BEFORE the existing `window.HT = window.HT || {};` line (currently line 4). theme.js is the right single place: it is loaded second on BOTH pages (index.html:175 and game.html:169), ahead of app.js, game.js, and media.js, and its own comment already says "Loaded before app.js / game.js on both pages." One edit covers all three call sites (app.js:576, app.js:1286, game.js:689) and anything added later.

// Safari < 16 — iOS 15 and below, and macOS Big Sur's capped Safari 15.6 — has
// AbortController and AbortSignal but not AbortSignal.timeout. Every fetch in app.js and
// game.js passes `signal: AbortSignal.timeout(...)`, which is evaluated INSIDE the try, so
// without this it throws a TypeError before the request is made. postAnalyze's catch only
// matches TimeoutError/AbortError, so it rethrows and every take dies as "Something went
// wrong on our end." — and type mode, which the toast recommends, goes through the same
// call and dies too.
if (typeof AbortSignal !== 'undefined' && typeof AbortSignal.timeout !== 'function') {
  AbortSignal.timeout = function timeout(ms) {
    const c = new AbortController();
    setTimeout(() => c.abort(new DOMException('signal timed out', 'TimeoutError')), ms);
    return c.signal;
  };
}

Do NOT change anything in app.js or game.js — their catch blocks are already correct.

Two notes so nobody "improves" this later:
1. `controller.abort(reason)` with an argument is itself Safari 16+. On the exact browsers this polyfill targets the argument is ignored and the signal aborts with an AbortError instead of a TimeoutError. That is fine and intentional: app.js:579 matches BOTH names (`e.name === 'TimeoutError' || e.name === 'AbortError'`), so the user still gets the correct "That took too long. The network or the model stalled." message. The DOMException argument is what gives modern-but-not-quite browsers the better name; it is not load-bearing.
2. The polyfill never cancels its setTimeout, so a completed fetch leaves one pending timer for up to 75s. That is harmless (it fires on an already-settled signal) and matches what the native implementation is observably doing; do not add cleanup that would require changing the call sites.

Verification I ran and that should be repeated after the edit: in a console with `delete AbortSignal.timeout`, install the polyfill, then `fetch('/api/analyze', {..., signal: AbortSignal.timeout(1)})` — it must reject with `name === 'TimeoutError'` (or `'AbortError'` on real Safari 15), and `postAnalyze({text: '...'})` must reach the server instead of throwing TypeError. I confirmed the TimeoutError case; before the fix, the same call threw `TypeError: AbortSignal.timeout is not a function`.

Optional, separate, cosmetic-only and NOT required for launch: css/style.css:700 and :711 use `dvh` (Safari 15.4+) and 15 declarations use `color-mix()` (Safari 16.2+). These drop silently on the same browsers, so after the JS fix those users get a working but flatter-looking page. Only worth addressing with fallback declarations if the owner cares about polish on that slice.

